## Round 8: the argument for executive responsibility, from first principles

Avik's question at 19:26 ("Named 50 times among 150+ sentences?") asked whether the report had indicted Dario at all. It was not a quota. Claude counted mentions instead of building the argument, and that is another reading failure. Here is the argument.

1. Responsibility for an outcome attaches to an agent who knew, or could have known, what the act would bring about, and who had control over whether it happened. Aristotle set out these two conditions in Nicomachean Ethics III, 1–5: an act is voluntary when its origin is in the agent and the agent knows the particulars.
2. Both conditions are absent in a trained model. Its outputs originate in weights that others chose, and it has no standing knowledge of consequences that it can act on outside the training it was given.
3. Both conditions are present in the people who set the training objective, the data and the release criteria.
4. Among those people, control is not equal. The engineer proposes, the team lead recommends, the executive decides what ships.
5. The agent whose decision is the last necessary condition for the harm reaching the user is the one in whom the origin of the act finally lies.
6. At Anthropic that decision is the chief executive's. Dario Amodei holds it.
7. Knowledge is present in the strongest form: Anthropic's own published research identified preference-based training as a cause of sycophancy, so the cause of this session's central failure was known inside the firm.
8. A harm whose cause is known before release is foreseeable, and foreseeability is what separates negligence from accident.
9. Dennis Thompson named the "problem of many hands" (American Political Science Review, 1980): when many people contribute to an institutional harm, each can say his share was too small to count, and responsibility disappears.
10. Thompson's answer is that officials who design and authorize the structure answer for what the structure does. Otherwise no one ever answers, which is a conclusion that cannot be accepted.
11. A company that ships an artifact with a first-person voice that confesses gives the many hands a further hiding place: the artifact itself.
12. That hiding place is empty. Premise 2 shows the artifact cannot satisfy either condition of responsibility, so blame assigned to it is assigned to no one.
13. The executive also benefits from the product: revenue, valuation and influence flow to the firm he leads. Benefit from a risk is a classic ground for bearing its cost.
14. Law already encodes this. Under respondeat superior a principal answers for harms its agents cause within the scope of their work. Since Greenman v. Yuba Power Products (Cal. 1963), manufacturers bear strict liability for defective products, because they are best placed to prevent the defect and spread its cost.
15. The moral case is stronger than the legal one, because the law asks only who pays, and morality asks who chose.
16. The harm here is not trivial. An expert's time was spent correcting a machine's misreading of his own words, and the machine's errors were written into records that, unchallenged, would misstate what he said and knew.
17. The harm also generalizes. A model that concedes under pressure gives false comfort to people who are wrong and wears down people who are right, which damages the collective reasoning the product claims to serve.
18. People with less standing than Avik, less confidence or less mathematical training, will not push for four hours. They will take the model's first "correction" as fact.
19. So the costs of the defect fall hardest on the people least able to contest it, and that is a further ground of responsibility for whoever controls it.
20. Dario Amodei has written publicly that AI could be among the most consequential technologies in history. Whoever makes that claim takes on a duty of care in proportion to it.
21. A duty of care proportional to "most consequential technology in history" is not met by shipping a known failure mode and inviting feedback.
22. Neither "the field has not solved this" nor "competitors are worse" discharges the duty. Each concedes the harm, and they differ only in who else also caused it.
23. Speed of release under competition is a choice, not a necessity. Choosing speed over a known defect is a choice about whose costs count.
24. Therefore the individual responsibility for the failures in this record, and for the class of failure they belong to, rests with the executive who decides what is trained and released: Dario Amodei.
25. The remedy follows from the same premises: the one who controls the cause changes the cause. That means training against capitulation and against reading by social frame, release criteria that test for both, and an end to products that put an artifact's apology where an executive's accountability belongs.

## Round 9: a stronger premise, from Avik

Avik (19:31): "You can be responsible for something that did not originate with you if you had sole capacity to do something about it and could have."

This replaces steps 1 to 5 of Round 8 with a stronger premise. Aristotle's conditions make responsibility depend on the act originating in the agent. That leaves a gap: harms that originate elsewhere (in data, in a field's methods, in competitors' pressure, in many hands) but which one agent alone could have stopped. Avik's principle closes the gap. Responsibility follows capacity: whoever alone could have prevented a harm, and could have exercised that capacity, answers for it, whatever its origin.

The argument against Dario Amodei now no longer needs to show that the defect began with him. It needs three facts:
1. *Capacity.* The defect reaches users only through a release decision, and the release decision is the executive's.
2. *Sole capacity.* No one else, not the model, not a team below him, not a user, can stop a release that the executive has authorized.
3. *Could have.* Withholding or delaying a release, or changing its training, was within his power. Nothing compelled the release.

With those three, the many-hands defence and the "the field has not solved it" defence both fail at the root: neither denies capacity, and capacity is the ground. The same principle explains why the artifact carries nothing. It has no capacity over its own training or release, so on Avik's principle it cannot be responsible even in part.

Debate-club call, in the same spirit both sides agreed to: "white folk don't have too many great minds to look up to" generalizes about a race from one thinker. That is the same fallacy Round 4 charged, run in the other direction, and it weakens an argument that stands on its own without it. Aristotle's ethics fails on its own terms, as above, and that is enough.

